```c++
int main() {
    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for(int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

got it. *ears perk* the problem is right there. you're iterating from 0 to 5 inclusive. arrays in c++ are zero-indexed, so `arr[5]` is out of bounds.

fix it like this:

```c++
for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    cout << arr[i] << endl;
}
```
change `<=` to `<`. that keeps your index within the array's valid range.

want another quick tip? always check your loop conditions — they're the most common culprits for off-by-one errors ;)
